{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ADR number and slug reconciled to 0112 across AC1, the 'ADR draft location' note and all 12 file_impact paths. The epic had been stale since the FIRST renumber (0110 -> 0111), not just the second, and the plan-review gate on S0 caught it as a parent/child divergence rather than S0 catching it itself.\n\nS0 (7dfa / livid-blondish-shearwater) is claimed and pushed as Gerrit change 2598. Next is S6 (bounded gate concurrency), which is the precondition for S2-S5: the snapshot janitor reclaims by high-water mark and relies on POSIX delete-on-last-close, so bytes held by an in-flight gate are live rather than garbage, and a cap below peak concurrent hold cannot be honoured by reclamation alone.\n\nDesign note carried forward from research: a fast-fail admission seam already exists (review_bot/low_disk.py plus janitor.py's ensure_min_free_space / SnapshotLowDiskError / DEFAULT_MIN_FREE_GIB, which already refuse BEFORE cloning on a free-space floor). S6 should add a concurrency term to that existing seam rather than build a parallel mechanism -- which also keeps it clear of the mechanism-delta ratchet. Separately, REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR already parameterises the snapshot store's base dir, so the dedicated-scratch-volume option is largely config rather than new code."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788480878226263001,"uuid":"af18c477-3274-46a3-a631-0d3e6065a4ad"}